data: {"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"**Redis** is fast, and per-key TTLs suit carts that should expire when abandoned. It also adds a service to run, needs persistence and memory limits configured so carts aren't evicted, and is harder to query for analytics like abandoned-cart reports. **Postgres** is durable and transactional, adds no new infrastructure if you already run it, and makes carts queryable alongside orders. Its costs are slightly higher latency per read and a scheduled job to delete stale carts. Both fix the cookie problem the same way: the cookie holds only a small session ID, and the cart lives server-side. I'd recommend **Postgres**, since a lost cart is lost revenue, cart traffic is modest, and you avoid a new piece of infrastructure. Choose Redis instead if you already operate it well and treat carts as disposable."}}

